  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1229 AM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nModels are finally in agreement with the evolution of the upper\npattern, with the GFS trending towards the ECMWF and Canadian\nsolutions. Still have a feeling the NBM guidance is slow to\nrespond for afternoon temperatures by later this week.\n\nMonday...the upper high over the southeast states will\nretrograde west towards Texas, with upper ridging starting to\nbuild north across Colorado. One last upper wave will drop\nsoutheast across the state Monday afternoon which will lead to\nshower and thunderstorm development for the Eastern Mountains\nand Plains. PWAT values begin to dry out as the upper ridge\nbuild, which will impact rainfall rates. Southeasterly flow on\nthe Plains will keep dewpoints elevated and shear weaken\noverhead with the building ridge. Thunderstorms look to develop\nalong the Eastern Mountains by early afternoon, and spread\nsoutheast across the Plains through the evening. Locally heavy\nrainfall, lightning, wind gusts to 50 mph and half inch hail\nwill likely accompany stronger storms. We will continue to\nmonitor the need for Flash Flood Watches for area burn scars.\nThis activity will spread south and east during the evening, and\nclear the Plains by Tuesday morning. Temperatures will remain\nmild on Monday with highs in the 80s to near 90 by the Kansas\nborder.\n\nTuesday through Sunday...the upper ridge will develop into a\nstrong upper high centered across Utah and Colorado through this\nperiod. Drier air will spread across the region, and much drier\nconditions expected through the period. Daily shower and\nthunderstorm activity is forecast to develop across the\nMountains, and spread east into the I-25 corridor through the\nevenings before dissipating overnight. Afternoon high\ntemperatures will climb back through the 90s on Tuesday across\nthe Plains. A system passing well to the north will send a cold\nfront for Wednesday with a brief cool down along with the best\nchances for showers and thunderstorms during this period. Model\nguidance really cranks the high overhead from Thursday through\nSunday. NBM temperature guidance has warmed a bit, but still\nlower than ensemble and deterministic guidance.",
